INV-midag's haskell row: 119 of 3,012 call edges on a 26-repo run fell outside
their src's span, and none of those srcs shared a name with another function.
The row said so ("a different mechanism") and left it uninvestigated.

The mechanism: a haskell function is written as consecutive EQUATIONS, one per
pattern, and each is its own `function`/`bind` node. add_symbol emits one
symbol per NAME from the FIRST equation, so the span ended where the second
began (xmonad's `handle`: 287-292, while its equations run to 414). The
enclosing lookup is by name, and haskell forbids a second top-level definition
of a name, so every call was anchored to the RIGHT function. Its span was
simply too short. This is the one INV-midag language where the anchor was
correct and the span was not, so the position-keyed lookup is not adopted here.
It would miss every equation after the first.

FIX: a pre-pass records each module-level name's last equation end, and a
function symbol's span runs from its first equation to its last. Haskell
requires a function's equations to be adjacent, so that range is exactly the
function. `line_span` follows the span. `cyclomatic_complexity` is still
computed over the first equation only. That predates this change, and changing
it would move a separate measurement.

MEASURED, surveys from a pinned worktree:
  out-of-span   haskell 119 -> 0 (xmonad, hadolint); 3,012 calls checked in both
  edge sets     every lost edge has a gained twin at the same line with the same
                dst name (29 on xmonad, 34 on hadolint, 0 unpaired). A symbol id
                embeds its end line, so a multi-equation function's id changes,
                and so does every edge that names it.

Addresses INV-midag.

